packets.implementation:
**Mark, do not delete.** A browser route is a real node (`where is /dashboard rendered?` is a legitimate query); only the HTTP pairing index must exclude it. Use the existing `cell_type::ORIGIN` (CellTypeId(15), code-domain:352) — no new CellType id, no ALL-table entry, no registry churn. The provenance vocabulary is already open-ended (engine:1448/1487 write `"provenance":"documentation"`, which is not in the doc-comment list either), and engram-export:101 passes the string straight through.

1. In react.rs, angular.rs, vue.rs: alongside the existing ROUTE_METHOD cell push a second cell
   `Cell { kind: cell_type::ORIGIN, payload: CellPayload::Json(r#"{"provenance":"nav_route"}"#.to_string()) }`
   and increment a local `nav_routes: usize`. Add `pub nav_routes: usize` to `ReactNodes` (react.rs:15), `AngularNodes` (angular.rs:14) and `VueNodes` (vue.rs:11) and set it in the three struct literals (react.rs:83, angular.rs:64, vue.rs:96). Verified: nothing else in the workspace constructs these structs, so the field is additive.
2. In dart: give `emit_dart_route` (dart:764) a `nav: bool` parameter; pass `true` from the `GoRoute(` branch (dart:681) and `false` from the shelf `.get(`/`.post(` branch (dart:701). When `nav`, push the same ORIGIN cell. Dart's counter rides on `Acc` and is not surfaced (dart parse has no stats channel) — the graph-side counter below covers it.
3. In graph/src/lib.rs add
   ```rust
   /// A ROUTE node tagged `provenance: nav_route` by a client-router extractor
   /// (react-router / Angular Router / vue-router / go_router). It is a browser
   /// navigation target, not a server endpoint, so it must never be an
   /// HTTP_CALLS target. Cheap substring test — the payload is written by us,
   /// not user JSON, matching `extract_method_field`'s existing tight scan.
   fn is_nav_route(cells: &[Cell]) -> bool {
       cells.iter().any(|c| c.kind == cell_type::ORIGIN
           && matches!(&c.payload, CellPayload::Json(j) | CellPayload::Text(j)
                       if j.contains("\"provenance\":\"nav_route\"")))
   }
   ```
   and in `build_route_index`'s loop (graph:1177-1188) `if is_nav_route(&n.cells) { excluded += 1; continue; }` before the `index_route_node` call. After the loop `eprintln!("[http] nav-routes excluded from route index: {excluded}");`.
4. engine: add `macro_rules! run_counted` next to `run!` (engine:873) that also does `nav_routes += out.nav_routes;`, use it for the react/angular/vue calls (engine:922/933/944), thread the total out of `apply_cross_cutting_extractors` (change its return type from `()` to `usize`, or accumulate into a `&mut ExtractStats` out-param — the out-param is preferable because A3.5 adds a second counter), and print `eprintln!("[extract] nav-routes marked: {n}")` once per build beside the `[incremental]` marker (engine:395).

No unwrap/panic anywhere; `matches!` guards every payload read.

