packets.implementation:
Rewrite `normalise_segment` to fold four more shapes, keeping the existing three byte-identical:
```rust
/// Collapse one path segment to `{}` if it is a parameter in ANY framework's
/// syntax. Deliberately conservative on `*`: a NAMED splat (`*path`, Rails /
/// gorilla) is a parameter, but a bare `*` or `**` is a catch-all that would
/// match every sibling path, so it stays literal and unpairable.
fn normalise_segment(seg: &str) -> String {
    let is_param = seg.starts_with(':')                                   // :id, :id?
        || (seg.starts_with('{') && seg.ends_with('}'))                   // {id}, {id:int}, {path:.*}
        || (seg.starts_with('<') && seg.ends_with('>'))                   // <int:uid>, <path:sub>, <id>
        || (seg.starts_with('[') && seg.ends_with(']'))                   // [id], [...slug]
        || (seg.starts_with('*') && seg.len() > 1 && seg != "**")         // *path (named splat only)
        || seg.contains("${");                                            // template interpolation
    if is_param { "{}".to_string() } else { seg.to_string() }
}
```
That is the whole product change. Guard the `[` arm against the empty-bracket edge (`seg.len() > 2`) so a literal `[]` segment is not folded.

Marker: extend `HttpMatchStats` (introduced in A3.1) with `folds_angle/folds_splat/folds_bracket`, counted in `build_route_index` and in `resolve`'s endpoint loop via a tiny `fn new_fold_kinds(raw: &str) -> (usize, usize, usize)` that classifies segments BEFORE normalisation, and print a second line from `resolve`: `eprintln!("[http] placeholder folds: angle={} splat={} bracket={}", ...)`. This is why the packet depends on A3.1 — it reuses that stats struct and would otherwise conflict in the same function.

Extend graph:2708-2717 with `assert_eq!(normalise_http_path("/users/<int:uid>"), "/users/{}")`, `"/files/<path:sub>"`, `"/files/*path"` → `/files/{}`, `"/blog/[slug]"` and `"/blog/[...slug]"` → `/blog/{}`, and the NEGATIVE cases `assert_eq!(normalise_http_path("/static/*"), "/static/*")` and `"/a/**"` unchanged.

