```rust
fn resolve_file(&self, file: &str) -> Vec<NodeId> {
    let want = file.trim().trim_start_matches("./").replace('\\', "/");
    let mut precise = Vec::new();
    let mut loose = Vec::new();
    for g in &self.graphs {
        for n in &g.nodes {
            if let Some((f, _, _)) = position_of(n) {
                if path_tail_matches(&f, &want) { precise.push(n.id); }
                else if basename(&f) == basename(&want) { loose.push(n.id); }
            }
        }
    }
    let mut out = if precise.is_empty() { loose } else { precise };
    out.sort_by_key(|id| id.0);
    out
}
```
The basename pass is kept as a fallback so a bare `utils.py` query (no directory) behaves exactly as today, and so a query whose prefix differs from the POSITION prefix (a repo-root-relative vs package-relative mismatch) still finds something rather than nothing. `resolve_frame` gets the same two-pass shape, keeping the narrowest-span tie-break within each pass and only falling to the basename pass when the precise pass found no spanning node.

Marker, emitted from resolve_signal (not from the two helpers, which are called per line): accumulate counts across the arm and emit once — `eprintln!("[resolve] file signal: path-suffix={precise} basename-fallback={loose}");` when the total is non-zero.

No caller changes: both functions keep their signatures.

Replace the MODULE-only gate with a one-level descent through PACKAGE children. Keep the node's own `confidence` (currently taken from the MODULE node, `n.confidence` at :1600) as the confidence carried into the index — for a PACKAGE-nested type, use the enclosing MODULE's confidence, unchanged, so the `weakest()` pairing at :1618 behaves identically:
```rust
for n in &g.nodes {
    if g.nav.kind_by_id.get(&n.id) != Some(&node_kind::MODULE) { continue; }
    // A type can hang off the file MODULE directly (Python/TS/Go) or off a
    // namespace PACKAGE inside it (C# `namespace`, PHP `namespace`, Ruby
    // `module`) — descend exactly one level so namespaced DTOs are indexed too.
    let mut scopes: Vec<NodeId> = vec![n.id];
    if let Some(children) = g.nav.children_of.get(&n.id) {
        for &c in children {
            if g.nav.kind_by_id.get(&c) == Some(&node_kind::PACKAGE) { scopes.push(c); }
        }
    }
    for scope in scopes {
        let Some(children) = g.nav.children_of.get(&scope) else { continue };
        for &child in children {
            if let Some(qname) = g.nav.qname_by_id.get(&child)
                && is_schema_type(qname, g.nav.kind_by_id.get(&child).copied())
            {
                import_index.entry(g.nav.name_by_id.get(&child).cloned().unwrap_or_default())
                    .or_default().push((child, g.repo, n.confidence));
            }
        }
    }
}
```
One level only — deeper nesting (a type inside a type) is not a shared-schema shape and unbounded recursion here would explode the pairwise emission.

