```rust
/// Repo-relative path a node should be scoped by. POSITION first (the normal
/// case), then the ENDPOINT_HIT cell's `file` for synthetic ENDPOINT nodes that
/// carry no span. Returns None for nodes with neither — ROUTE nodes included,
/// because ROUTE_METHOD is a bare CellPayload::Text holding only the HTTP verb
/// (parsers/code/csharp/src/lib.rs:531-535 and the same in go/dart/elixir/clojure).
fn scope_file_of(merged: &MergedGraph, id: NodeId) -> Option<String>;

/// True when `file` is inside `scope`. Prefix match on a `/` boundary only, so
/// scope="services/ap" does NOT match "services/api/handler.py". Both sides are
/// normalised by trimming leading "./" and "/" and trailing "/".
fn in_scope(file: &str, scope: &str) -> bool {
    let f = file.trim_start_matches("./").trim_start_matches('/');
    let s = scope.trim_start_matches("./").trim_matches('/');
    if s.is_empty() { return true; }
    f == s || f.starts_with(&format!("{s}/"))
}
```
`scope_file_of` reuses `locate_node`'s POSITION walk; for the ENDPOINT_HIT fallback, find the node's cell with `kind == cell_type::ENDPOINT_HIT` and read `file` out of the JSON (shape verified at parsers/code/typescript/src/lib.rs:1136-1148: `{method,path,file,line,col,confidence}`).

POLICY, and it must be stated in the doc comment of every scoped fn: a node with NO locatable file is KEPT, never dropped. Dropping unlocatable nodes would silently delete every ENDPOINT/ROUTE/DOC_SPACE from a scoped answer and destroy the cross-service result that is the whole point of these primitives. The marker reports the kept-unlocatable count so the behaviour is visible rather than surprising.

One shared applier so the three call sites cannot drift:
```rust
fn apply_scope<T>(merged: &MergedGraph, items: Vec<T>, scope: Option<&str>, id_of: impl Fn(&T) -> NodeId, what: &str) -> Vec<T> {
    let Some(s) = scope else { return items };
    let before = items.len();
    let mut unlocatable = 0usize;
    let out: Vec<T> = items.into_iter().filter(|it| match scope_file_of(merged, id_of(it)) {
        Some(f) => in_scope(&f, s),
        None => { unlocatable += 1; true }
    }).collect();
    eprintln!("[scope] {what} scope={s}: {before} -> {} (unlocatable={unlocatable})", out.len());
    out
}
```
