   - `let idx = source.find(pattern)?;` (same first-occurrence semantics as `extract_topic_near` at :196 — deliberately consistent with today's precision level; A2 owns multi-occurrence).
   - Window: `const BACK: usize = 600; const FWD: usize = 400;` — `let mut lo = idx.saturating_sub(BACK); while lo > 0 && !source.is_char_boundary(lo) { lo -= 1; }` and symmetrically `let mut hi = (idx + pattern.len() + FWD).min(source.len()); while hi < source.len() && !source.is_char_boundary(hi) { hi += 1; }`. No unwrap, no panic path.
   - Try `scan_generic(&source[lo..hi])`, then `scan_go_struct(&source[lo..hi])`, each returning the EARLIEST byte match so the result is order-independent; on both misses retry over the whole `source` with `window = "file"`. Return `None` if still nothing.

4. `fn scan_generic(s: &str) -> Option<(String, String)>`: for each wrapper take `s.find(w)`, keep the minimum index, then call `last_type_arg(&s[i + w.len()..])`.
   `fn last_type_arg(s: &str) -> Option<String>`: depth-1 scan over `s.as_bytes()` — `b'<'` depth += 1; `b'>'` depth -= 1 and at 0 return `s[seg..i]`; `b','` at depth 1 sets `seg = i + 1`; `b'\n'` returns None (never cross a line); bail past 512 bytes. All split points are ASCII so every `&s[a..b]` lands on a char boundary — state this in a comment, because CODE_RULES forbids the panicking alternative.

5. `fn scan_go_struct(s: &str) -> Option<(String, String)>`: find each `{`, walk backwards over `[A-Za-z0-9_.]` to the identifier start, require at least one `.`, and require the preceding char to be a non-identifier (`&`, space, `(`, `,`, `=`, start-of-window). Reject when the package segment is in `const GO_PKG_DENY: &[&str] = &["nats","kafka","sarama","bytes","http","sync","time","strings","errors","sql","json","url","os","io","fmt","context","tls"]` or the type segment is in `const GO_TYPE_DENY: &[&str] = &["Message","Msg","ProducerMessage","ConsumerMessage","Reader","Writer","Config","Client","Conn","Options"]` — otherwise `&kafka.Message{Topic:…}` would be mistaken for the payload.

6. `fn simple_type(raw: &str) -> Option<String>`: trim; strip a trailing `?` and any `[]`; take the segment after the last `.` or `::`; require `[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*` and len ≤ 64; return None otherwise. `Null`/`Ignore`/`string` are NOT rejected — by the Kafka generic convention the last arg is the value type, and rejecting them would silently drop `Message<Null, string>`; the report can weigh them.

7. `fn message_type_cell(source: &str, pattern: &str) -> Option<Cell>` builds `Cell { kind: cell_type::MESSAGE_TYPE, payload: CellPayload::Json(serde_json::json!({"type": simple, "raw": raw, "form": form, "window": window}).to_string()) }` (serde_json is already a dependency of this crate).

8. At :152 and :183 replace `cells: vec![]` with `cells: message_type_cell(source, pattern).into_iter().collect()`. Nothing else in the node construction changes — same NodeId, same qname, same confidence, same nav.record.

