Add `pub mod data_entity` to code-domain/src/lib.rs (no new deps; code-domain already has zero tree-sitter deps and every parser + the extractors crate depends on it, so it is the only place all five emitters can share).

```rust
pub mod data_entity {
    /// Split a DATA_ENTITY qname into its join key `(flavor, canonical_name)`.
    /// A qname carrying the `data_entity:<flavor>:` prefix yields that flavor;
    /// a prefix-less qname (Java's bare @Entity simple name — see A13.2) is
    /// read as `"sql"`, the only flavor that emitter can currently mean.
    pub fn join_key(qname: &str) -> (&'static str, String);
    /// Idempotent fold of a surface entity name to its canonical form.
    pub fn canonical_entity_name(name: &str) -> String;
}
```
`canonical_entity_name` steps, in order: (1) drop a schema/owner qualifier — keep the text after the last `.` (`public.users` → `users`); (2) split camel/Pascal humps and rejoin with `_` (`UserAccount` → `User_Account`), treating an acronym run as one hump (`APIKey` → `API_Key`); (3) ASCII-lowercase; (4) singularise the LAST `_`-segment. `singularise`: `ies`→`y`; `sses|shes|ches|xes|zes`→ drop `es`; a trailing `s` NOT preceded by `s`, `u` or `i` → drop it; otherwise unchanged. That leaves `address`, `status`, `analysis`, `data` alone and folds `users`→`user`, `categories`→`category`, `boxes`→`box`. The fold only has to be IDEMPOTENT and applied to both sides — a wrong-but-consistent fold (`series`→`sery`) still joins correctly; the only real risk is a false join between two genuinely different names that fold together, which the `(flavor, _)` half of the key already bounds (a Mongo `Post` never joins a SQL `posts`). Unit-test idempotency: `canonical(canonical(x)) == canonical(x)` over a table of ~20 names.

In `DbResolver::resolve`, change `entity_index` to `HashMap<(&'static str, String), Vec<(NodeId, RepoId, Confidence)>>` and key each node with `data_entity::join_key(qname)`. Leave the `refs.len() < 2` / `repos.len() < 2` / `refs[i].1 != refs[j].1` cross-repo gates exactly as they are — the change is the key, not the pairing rule. Add `const MAX_ENTITY_FANOUT: usize = 12;` above the impl: if a bucket's distinct-RepoId count exceeds it, `continue` and bump a `skipped` counter (pairs are O(n^2); a bucket like `user` across 30 services would mint 435 edges through a category that IS in `blast_carry_edges()`). Count `buckets`, `paired`, `skipped_fanout`, and `prefixless` (buckets whose key came from a qname with no `data_entity:` prefix — A13.2's proof), then `eprintln!` the marker once at the end of `resolve`. The skip decision is per-bucket and order-independent, so HashMap iteration order does not affect the edge set; `post_passes` (engine/src/lib.rs:519-522) already sorts `cross_edges`, so byte-determinism is preserved.

No `unwrap()`: `join_key` uses `strip_prefix` + `split_once` with `else` fallbacks; `rsplit('.').next()` is infallible on a non-empty &str but guard the empty-string case by returning the input unchanged.

Add two unit tests beside graph/src/lib.rs:3090-3126 using the existing `graph_with_entity` helper: `db_resolver_joins_bare_java_name_to_prefixed_table` (repo A qname `User`, repo B qname `data_entity:sql:users` → exactly one SHARES_DATA_ENTITY edge) and `db_resolver_does_not_join_across_flavors` (`data_entity:sql:users` vs `data_entity:nosql:User` → zero edges — this guards the existing assertion at graph/src/lib.rs:3376-3385 which must keep passing).

