packets.implementation:
New `doc-sources/src/filter.rs` holding the whole matcher, pure and offline:

```rust
/// Title filter for `glia docs sync`. Patterns are case-insensitive; `*` is the
/// only metacharacter (matches any run of characters). A pattern without `*` is
/// a plain substring test, so `--include payments` works unadorned.
pub struct TitleFilter { include: Vec<String>, exclude: Vec<String> }
impl TitleFilter {
    pub fn new(include: &[String], exclude: &[String]) -> Self;  // lowercases both lists
    /// true = keep. Empty include = keep everything not excluded.
    /// Any exclude hit wins over every include hit.
    pub fn keep(&self, title: &str) -> bool;
    pub fn is_active(&self) -> bool;  // !include.is_empty() || !exclude.is_empty()
}
fn glob_match(pat: &str, hay: &str) -> bool;  // both already lowercased
```
`glob_match`: if `!pat.contains('*')` → `hay.contains(pat)`. Otherwise split on '*'; the first segment must be a prefix unless the pattern starts with '*', the last segment must be a suffix unless it ends with '*', and the middle segments must occur in order (walk a cursor with `hay[cursor..].find(seg)`). Empty segments from `**` or leading/trailing '*' are skipped. No regex, no new dependency.

Wire into `doc-sources/src/lib.rs`: `pub mod filter;` and `pub use filter::TitleFilter;` alongside the existing snapshot re-exports.

CLI, in the `Sync` variant:
```rust
/// Keep only pages whose title matches. Repeatable; `*` wildcard; case-insensitive.
#[arg(long, value_name = "PATTERN")] include: Vec<String>,
/// Drop pages whose title matches. Repeatable; wins over --include.
#[arg(long, value_name = "PATTERN")] exclude: Vec<String>,
```
In the handler, immediately after `pull_space` succeeds:
```rust
let filter = TitleFilter::new(&include, &exclude);
let fetched = pages.len();
let pages: Vec<_> = pages.into_iter().filter(|p| filter.keep(&p.title)).collect();
eprintln!("[docs] sync space={space} fetched={fetched} kept={} include={} exclude={}",
          pages.len(), include.len(), exclude.len());
if pages.is_empty() && fetched > 0 {
    eprintln!("error: every one of {fetched} fetched page(s) was filtered out; refusing to overwrite the snapshot with an empty manifest");
    return 1;
}
```
The refuse-on-empty guard matters because `write_snapshot` (snapshot.rs:60-71) truncates `manifest.jsonl` unconditionally, so an over-broad `--exclude` would otherwise silently wipe a good snapshot and the next `glia build` would drop every doc node. No `unwrap()`/`panic!()` anywhere; the handler keeps its existing `return 1` / `return 2` exit-code discipline.

```rust
/// Doc→code mention index. Tier 1 keys the last two qname segments so a
/// qualified mention (`Class.method`) binds to the right member; tiers 2/3 keep
/// today's bare-name lookup but grade it by ambiguity instead of silently
/// picking the lowest NodeId.
struct DocSymbolIndex {
    by_name:  HashMap<String, (NodeId, usize)>,  // name        -> (lowest id, count)
    by_tail2: HashMap<String, (NodeId, usize)>,  // "A::b"       -> (lowest id, count)
}
```
Built in one pass over `merged.graphs` × `g.nodes`, gated by the existing `is_doc_linkable_symbol(kind)` (engine:597-609). For each node: bump/insert `by_name[name]` keeping the lower id and incrementing the count; if the qname has ≥2 `::` segments, do the same for `by_tail2[last_two_segments]`. Both maps are only ever LOOKED UP, never iterated, so CODE_RULES §3 (no HashMap iteration) holds.

```rust
/// (target, confidence) for one inline-code span, or None.
fn resolve_doc_mention(span: &str, idx: &DocSymbolIndex) -> Option<(NodeId, Confidence)> {
    let norm = span.trim().trim_end_matches("()").replace('.', "::");
    // Tier 1 — qualified mention, e.g. `PaymentGateway.charge` / `mod::Thing`.
    if norm.contains("::") {
        let mut it = norm.rsplit("::");
        if let (Some(last), Some(prev)) = (it.next(), it.next())
            && is_identifier(prev) && is_identifier(last)
            && let Some(&(id, n)) = idx.by_tail2.get(&format!("{prev}::{last}"))
        {
            return Some((id, if n == 1 { Confidence::Strong } else { Confidence::Medium }));
        }
    }
    // Tiers 2/3 — bare tail name (today's behaviour), graded by ambiguity.
    let ident = identifier_from_span(span)?;
    let &(id, n) = idx.by_name.get(&ident)?;
    Some((id, if n == 1 { Confidence::Medium } else { Confidence::Weak }))
}
```
`is_identifier` carries the same rules `identifier_from_span` applies today — ≥3 chars, first char ascii-alpha or `_`, rest ascii-alphanumeric or `_` — so tier 1 can never match something today's path would have rejected.

The emission loop becomes `for span in backtick_spans(text)` → `resolve_doc_mention` → the existing `sym != n.id && seen.insert(sym)` guard → push `Edge { category: DOCUMENTS, confidence }` → the existing `MAX_LINKS_PER_DOC` break. Tally `strong`/`medium`/`weak` and, right before `merged.cross_edges.extend(new_edges)` (:593):
```rust
eprintln!("[doclink] {} DOCUMENTS edges (strong={strong} qualified, medium={medium} unique, weak={weak} ambiguous) over {doc_sections} doc sections", new_edges.len());
```
`post_passes` already sorts `cross_edges` afterwards (engine:521-523) so emission order stays irrelevant.

`bench/doc-link/check.sh`: change line 9 to `… analyze bench/doc-link/fixture-repo --format json 2>"$err" > "$tmp"` and add a grep asserting the marker contains `strong=2` and `weak=0` — verified against the fixture, whose two qualified mentions are `PaymentGateway.charge` and `OrderService.place_order` (bench/doc-link/pages/ordering.xhtml:5-7) and whose three bare mentions (`OrderService`, `PaymentGateway`, `get_user`) are each unique in bench/doc-link/fixture-repo.

In `code-domain/src/lib.rs`, next to `library_names`:
```rust
/// Segment-aligned index of everything this repo DECLARES. A candidate library
/// name that hits it is an intra-repo reference, not a dependency (audit #12 —
/// the Go carve-out at library_name's "go" arm generalised to every language).
#[derive(Default)]
pub struct LocalModuleIndex {
    /// Every segment-aligned prefix AND suffix of every MODULE/PACKAGE qname,
    /// `::`-joined. `src::snapshot` contributes {src, src::snapshot, snapshot}.
    paths: std::collections::BTreeSet<String>,
    /// Bare names of repo-declared top-level symbols → how many nodes declare
    /// each (ambiguity guard, mirroring graph::unique_global_function:747-761).
    symbols: std::collections::BTreeMap<String, usize>,
}
impl LocalModuleIndex {
    pub fn add_parse(&mut self, fp: &FileParse);
    pub fn is_local_path(&self, lib: &str) -> bool;    // normalise . and / to ::, then lookup
    pub fn is_local_symbol(&self, name: &str) -> bool; // count == 1
}
pub fn library_names_filtered(imports: &[ImportStmt], lang: &str,
                              local: &LocalModuleIndex) -> Vec<String>;
pub fn attach_imports_cell_filtered(fp: &mut FileParse, lang: &str,
                                    local: &LocalModuleIndex) -> (usize, usize);
```
`add_parse` walks `fp.nav` (kind_by_id / qname_by_id / name_by_id): kind ∈ {MODULE, PACKAGE} expands the qname into its prefixes and suffixes into `paths`; kind ∈ {MODULE, PACKAGE, CLASS, STRUCT, INTERFACE, ENUM, FUNCTION} bumps `symbols[name]`. The nav maps are HashMaps, but they are only READ to fill BTree-backed collections whose contents are order-independent, so CODE_RULES §3 holds and the result is deterministic.

`library_names_filtered` repeats `library_names`' loop (including its `level > 0` skip and its `.take(10)` cap, applied AFTER filtering so a file whose first ten names were all local still reports its real deps) and drops a candidate when either:
 (a) `local.is_local_path(candidate)` — catches rust (`snapshot` is a suffix of `src::snapshot`), python (`myapp` is a prefix of `myapp::auth`), php (`App` is a prefix of the PACKAGE `App::Services`), c_cpp (`myproj` is a prefix of `myproj::foo`);
 (b) the import is `ImportTarget::Symbol { name, .. }` and `local.is_local_symbol(name)` — this is the Java/C#/Scala case, where NO PACKAGE node exists (verified: the java-spring-imports fixture's only nodes are `Helper`, `Main`, their CLASSes and METHODs) so rule (a) cannot see `com::example::util`; `Helper` is a uniquely-declared repo CLASS, which settles it.

`attach_imports_cell_filtered` does `for n in &mut fp.nodes { n.cells.retain(|c| c.kind != cell_type::IMPORTS); }` before pushing the new cell, so it is idempotent whether the FileParse came fresh from a parser or warm from a cache written by an older binary.

Engine post-pass, inserted at :410:
```rust
// audit #12: an import that resolves inside this repo is not a dependency.
let mut local = repo_graph_code_domain::LocalModuleIndex::default();
for (_, parses) in &parses_by_lang { for fp in parses { local.add_parse(fp); } }
let (mut kept, mut dropped) = (0usize, 0usize);
for (lang, parses) in parses_by_lang.iter_mut() {
    for fp in parses.iter_mut() {
        let (k, d) = repo_graph_code_domain::attach_imports_cell_filtered(fp, lang, &local);
        kept += k; dropped += d;
    }
}
eprintln!("[imports] local-filter: kept {kept}, dropped {dropped} intra-repo name(s) across {} language group(s)", parses_by_lang.len());
```
`proto_parses` is deliberately NOT fed to the index: those FileParses come from `extract_grpc_service_nodes` (engine:325-333) and register GRPC_SERVICE names that would over-match real library names. They carry no imports, so they need no filtering either.

In `projection-text/src/lib.rs` (default feature set — do NOT put it behind `driver`, py does not enable that feature):
```rust
/// JSON string-body escaper for the hand-rolled projections (audit 2026-06-10
/// #16). Covers everything RFC 8259 requires: quote, backslash, and EVERY
/// control character below 0x20 — the four-`replace` version in py missed
/// 0x00-0x08, 0x0B, 0x0C and 0x0E-0x1F, any one of which makes `json.loads`
/// raise `Invalid control character`.
pub fn escape_json_string(s: &str) -> String {
    let mut out = String::with_capacity(s.len() + 8);
    for c in s.chars() {
        match c {
            '"'      => out.push_str("\\\""),
            '\\'     => out.push_str("\\\\"),
            '\n'     => out.push_str("\\n"),
            '\r'     => out.push_str("\\r"),
            '\t'     => out.push_str("\\t"),
            '\u{08}' => out.push_str("\\b"),
            '\u{0c}' => out.push_str("\\f"),
            c if (c as u32) < 0x20 => { let _ = write!(out, "\\u{:04x}", c as u32); }
            c => out.push(c),
        }
    }
    out
}
```
`let _ = write!(…)` (never `unwrap()`): writing to a String is infallible but the Result must be consumed, and CODE_RULES forbids unwrap in non-test code.

In `py/src/lib.rs`, replace the body of the private `escape_json` with a one-line delegation `repo_graph_projection_text::escape_json_string(s)` so all five call sites stay untouched.

PyGraph:
```rust
struct PyGraph { merged: MergedGraph, parse_errors: Vec<String> }
// inside #[pymethods] impl PyGraph, next to node_count/edge_count:
/// Files this build could not parse, as `"<path>: <reason>"`, in walk order.
/// Empty for a graph loaded from a `.gmap` — parse state is not persisted.
#[getter]
fn parse_errors(&self) -> Vec<String> { self.parse_errors.clone() }
```
`generate`: keep the existing all-empty guard verbatim, then add the marker and move the vec:
```rust
if !result.parse_errors.is_empty() {
    eprintln!("[parse] {} file(s) failed to parse (see PyGraph.parse_errors)", result.parse_errors.len());
}
Ok(PyGraph { merged: result.merged, parse_errors: result.parse_errors })
```
`generate_many` gets the same two changes (it currently discards `result.parse_errors` at :400-403 with no guard at all). `load_from_gmap` gets `parse_errors: Vec::new()`.

```rust
/// Local `$var` → class-name bindings inside ONE function body. PHP's dominant
/// intra-file dispatch is `$x = new Thing(); $x->m();`, and the binding is a
/// same-file, same-scope syntactic fact — the parser can settle it exactly the
/// way it already settles `$this->m()` as SelfMethod (CLAUDE.md parser/graph
/// split: SelfMethod walks to the enclosing CLASS). Anything cross-file still
/// resolves in the graph crate.
fn local_receiver_types(func: TsNode, src: &[u8]) -> HashMap<String, String>
```
